<section class="tool-guide" aria-labelledby="google-docs-guide"><h2 id="google-docs-guide">How to move a Google Docs draft into WordPress</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Copy your selection.</strong> In Google Docs, select the article text you want to bring. Use Ctrl+C on Windows or ChromeOS, or Command+C on Mac. Copying a selection avoids bringing unrelated document material.</li><li><strong>Paste in the box above.</strong> Use Ctrl+V or Command+V. A supported formatted clipboard selection opens its conversion preview immediately. The capture box empties for the next selection; its original source stays in this tab only for re-preview. The article is unchanged.</li><li><strong>Inspect the conversion.</strong> Check the reading preview, exact Markdown, extracted title, and all notices. If a table has no marked header, explicitly choose the first-row header option and preview again. Plain-text-only pastes receive a warning and cannot recover missing formatting. If needed, copy again without using Paste without formatting, or download a Word .docx from Google Docs and use the file alternative.</li><li><strong>Apply after review.</strong> Acknowledge the changes. Replace the article after confirmation, or choose an insertion destination to combine it with existing writing. Insertions need an exact location preview. Existing valid writing is checkpointed before changes; export approval clears.</li><li><strong>Finish the post.</strong> Review the article and WordPress findings, then approve and copy or download the blocks. In a new, empty WordPress draft, put the title in its own title field and paste the body into the full post Code editor. Return to Visual editor, inspect every block, and handle image placeholders. Save as a draft, close and reopen the post, and confirm the blocks remain editable without unexpected-content warnings. Preview before publishing yourself.</li></ol>
<p class="small">Use the full post Code editor. Pasting into a Code block or Custom HTML block serves a different purpose. Themes, plugins, and editor versions can affect the result. <a href="/help/publishing-handoff/#wordpress">WordPress handoff steps</a>.</p>
<h2 id="copied-writing-limits">What stays and what changes?</h2>
<div class="article-table" role="region" aria-label="Copied writing conversion support, scroll horizontally if needed" tabindex="0"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Copied material</th><th scope="col">Converted article</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Headings, paragraphs, emphasis and safe web links</td><td>Supported structure becomes editable Markdown and WordPress blocks. An opening H1 can become the separate title. Links are not visited or verified.</td></tr><tr><td>Bullets, ordinary numbered lists and simple tables</td><td>Supported nesting and list starts are kept. Tables need a header choice, up to twelve columns and 100 data rows. Merged, nested, or irregular tables need simplification.</td></tr><tr><td>Images, fonts, colors and document layout</td><td>Images become visible placeholders; no image is downloaded. Layout and decorative styling are not retained. Add images in WordPress, or prepare an existing HTTPS image URL with the article image tool.</td></tr><tr><td>Comments, suggestions, footnotes and document history</td><td>This tool sees only what the clipboard supplies. It does not inspect or resolve the original document. Resolve suggestions and notes in your source and compare the converted result. A pasted selection is not a complete document backup.</td></tr><tr><td>Plain text only</td><td>A clearly labelled plain-text conversion with an acknowledgement. Punctuation stays literal; headings, Markdown formatting, and page details are not interpreted. Choose Markdown in Import format for intentional Markdown. Formatting absent from the clipboard cannot be reconstructed. Copy again with formatting or use a Word .docx.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Copied source is limited to 512 KiB and the converted article to 60,000 characters, including the escape characters needed to keep plain-text punctuation literal. Oversized conversions are rejected in full. Oversized or empty clipboard data keeps any previous preview. A conversion failure keeps the copied source for retry, for example after explicitly choosing table headers; any earlier preview remains visible but cannot be applied until a selection converts successfully. Current article writing is always kept until apply. Clear the import or apply it to discard the retained clipboard source. Pending conversions are excluded from private backups and article exports.</p>
<h2 id="copied-writing-questions">Common questions</h2>
<p>For plain-text-only pastes, table decisions and rejected checklists, use the <a href="/help/conversion-checks/#google-docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Docs troubleshooting walkthrough (new tab)</a>. Save current writing before navigating away.</p>
<details><summary>Does this read my Google account or document link?</summary><p>No. There is no Google account connection or URL import. Copy the writing you choose and paste it into the capture box. No clipboard permission is requested and no clipboard read occurs in the background.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why did my formatting disappear?</summary><p>The browser may supply only plain text, especially when using Paste without formatting. The preview identifies that case. Missing source formatting cannot be recovered here. Copy the selection again, or use File, Download in Google Docs and choose a Word .docx to open in the file alternative.</p></details>
<details><summary>Can I keep the current article and add this section?</summary><p>Yes. Choose Add to the end of the article or Insert at article cursor or replace selected blocks in the conversion preview. Inspect the exact insertion before applying. Existing page fields and private notes stay in place; imported page fields and questions are not merged.</p></details>
<details><summary>Will it look identical in WordPress?</summary><p>No. This is a limited article conversion and a manual handoff. Inspect all blocks, links, tables, image placeholders and destination settings in your actual WordPress editor before publishing.</p></details>
<p class="small">Official workflow references, checked October 14, 2026: <a href="https://support.google.com/docs/answer/161768?hl=en" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Docs copy and paste</a>, <a href="https://support.google.com/docs/answer/49114?hl=en" target="_blank" rel="noopener">download a document</a>, and <a href="https://wordpress.org/documentation/article/keyboard-shortcuts-block-editor/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WordPress editor controls</a>. These explain their products; they do not endorse or validate this independent tool.</p>
